From 19171c242750235d8be32d317d506e87fe9ba359 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 01:41:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Wayland: place a child window that stays on the bottom below
 its parent

A child QWindow is a subsurface of its parent's surface, and a new
subsurface is placed above its parent and its siblings
(wl_subsurface.place_above, place_below). A program that shows a picture
under its own window, which is transparent where the picture shows (the
lock screen's wallpaper under its interface, a video under its
controls), had no way to put a child window there: raise() and lower()
act on shell surfaces only.

A child window with Qt::WindowStaysOnBottomHint now lies below its
parent's own surface: QWaylandWindow::updateSubSurfaceStacking() asks the
subsurface for place_below(parent) when it is made and when the hint is
set, and for place_above(parent) when the hint is cleared again
(QWaylandSubSurface::setBelowParent()). The order is the parent's state,
so the parent is asked to draw, which commits it. A child window without
the hint is placed as before. The subsurface keeps the order, at the end
of its members, so QWaylandWindow's layout does not change for modules
built against its private header (LayerShellQt among them). The window's
dynamic property _q_wayland_below_parent says where it lies, so a
program can tell a Qt that does this from one that does not (where its
child would cover its window instead).

tst_surface::subsurfaceStaysOnBottom shows a parent with a child that
stays on the bottom and one that does not, and checks the requests the
compositor gets: place_below with the parent's surface for the first,
nothing for the second, and place_above with the parent's surface once
the hint is cleared, and the property each time. The mock compositor
records these requests.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
